Emotions are an important aspect in general human understanding as they lead to better understanding of conversation and other daily behavior. Many psychologists have defined rules for emotion detection like different movements of muscles in faces or significant changes in the voice while speaking. Therefore, many patterns were observed and aligned with a specific set of emotions.

Deep learning is a well researched topic, known for many kinds of pattern recognition. Nowadays, transformer models like ChatGPT are gaining more and more popularity and they are said to be all-purpose tools for many tasks. This thesis implements state-of-the art transformers for emotion detection in text, audio and video. These were not yet covered by research in emotion detection with artificial intelligence. Furthermore, it compares these with traditional convolution models and gets in touch with multimodal recognition, where the results are combined together through their labels. 
// Transformer wurden vorher noch nicht betrachet

The results show, that transformer architectures are able to recognize emotions. Classification in videos showed by far the most promising results. However, this thesis showed that emotions are context specific and that neither state-of-the art models nor the convolution models are able to recognize these beyond their dataset.
